{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789294197,"nonce":"su8O+ntm25dXmsLKCa+O4umvwpA=","title":"Python frontend errors bypassed the diagnostic renderer","message":"A parse error from the Python parser was printed as its raw message and\nbyte range (\"Expected an expression at 24..25\"), an unsupported form as\n\"X is not supported yet (at byte offset N)\", and the lowering's own\nwarnings rendered without a snippet because the program carried no\nsource file. None of it went through the Ariadne renderer the rest of\nthe compiler uses.\n\nFix: errors carry a span and the module they occurred in;\n`Error::render(file, source, colors)` produces the same report as the\ncompiler's diagnostics, and zypy prints that against the right file\n(the main one, or the imported module the error names). The program\nattaches its main source, so lowering diagnostics show their line.\nPython's unannotated parameters are marked `dynamic`, which W0001 takes\nas intended rather than missing, so a Python program no longer warns\non every def.\n\nLeft: a span inside an imported module cannot be told from one in the\nmain file by the compiler's source map, which has one file; a lowering\nwarning about a module's function would render against the main file's\ntext. Threading a file id through Span is the fix.","files":null}]}